- Route Danish corpus citations through a strict da-DK numeric grounding profile with decimal-comma rates, dot-grouped amounts, Danish percentage and currency markers, and legal-structure typing. The profile now uses an equal-length structural mask with identity provenance, snaps mask edges to numeric envelopes, and stops marker and unary-sign scans at mask boundaries. Standalone markers use a closed neighbor allowlist whose wrappers are transparent only when their full outward delimiter run is detached. Numeric continuation consumes only zero-width joiner, zero-width non-joiner, word joiner, soft hyphen, and byte-order mark inside one raw-exact numeral; other format characters act as token boundaries, and continuation shares the envelope grammar for decimal, grouping, and exponent forms, including failed exponent transitions and atomic malformed separator runs. Danish negative signs are joined only across those five format characters, so whitespace-prefixed prose negatives retain their sign while spaced range operators do not. Danish Markdown table-header context was removed: constructed pipe tables without an adjacent currency marker now fail closed, accepting the prior table-starvation case as intended fail-closed behavior. The 1,922-row live corpus contains no pipe characters, so the removal has zero live-corpus impact; the pre-existing 533/533 dot-group magnitude gate remains passing.
- Recognize the complete German disability-pension evidence condition in English-named formula witnesses, and keep the medical onset-age condition separate from the following independent effects requirement. Calendar witnesses remain subject to existing execution and source-coverage checks.
- Recognize the German age-relative body/health-condition deviation predicate when matching an English formula selector, requiring the complete condition and selector vocabulary and preserving source and selector negation.
