- Ground numbers that Hebrew statutory text writes as words. Israeli statutes name a number with a word far more often than with a digit: National Insurance Law section 68(b) sets one rate for the fourth child and another for the fifth, and section 68(c) a supplement for a parent entitled for three children or more, without printing 3, 4 or 5 anywhere, and Income Tax Ordinance section 34 grants two credit points and prints no 2 — so the CI numeric-grounding gate rejected correctly generated rules as ungrounded literals. Hebrew ordinals and cardinals now map to their values the way the English, French and Dutch words already do, allowing the one- or two-letter prefixes that bind to a Hebrew word (the definite article, the conjunction, the prepositions) with or without a maqaf, and refusing a match inside a longer Hebrew word. Eleven through nineteen are built from two words, unit then ten, and are matched as a pair before the single-word pass so that Income Tax Ordinance section 33A's "divided by twelve" grounds 12 rather than the 10 inside it.
- Ground numerals that Hebrew statutory text prints with a maqaf or a fraction slash. Hebrew attaches a one-letter prefix preposition to a following numeral with a maqaf (U+05BE, the Hebrew hyphen), and `_clean_source_text_for_numeric_extraction` had no rule for it: the Income Tax Ordinance section 121 rate schedule's `mem`-maqaf-84,120 was misread as the trailing "120" and National Insurance Law section 68(b)'s `bet`-maqaf-2.24 was dropped outright, so the CI grounding gate rejected bracket bounds and multipliers that are printed in the statute verbatim. Detach the maqaf before a digit the way the cedi, naira, and kwacha prefixes are already detached. Separately, recognise the Unicode fraction slash (U+2044): Income Tax Ordinance section 36 prints a quarter credit point as `1⁄4`, which the precomposed vulgar-fraction table cannot see. Contribute the fraction and its printed numerator and denominator, unguarded — U+2044 exists only to typeset fractions, so unlike the ASCII slash it needs no context test. A mixed number reaches the encoder from the corpus as the whole number, a space and the fraction (`2 1⁄2`, the form the corpus adapter keeps for OpenLaw's superscript-over-subscript typesetting) and is read as two and a half; a digit run glued to the slash (`21⁄2`) is read as the improper fraction it spells.
- A maqaf binds the Hebrew word before it -- a prefix stack or a word of a compound, beginning at a word boundary, so a maqaf inside an identifier such as "121א־2" binds nothing, carrying a unary sign before it, and a chain of maqaf-joined words with wrap space after any of its maqafs bound as one -- to the word or number after it across wrap space -- spaces of any kind or one line wrap, never a blank line -- in proof matching and in numeric extraction alike, by one shared pattern ("ל־ 1⁄2", "עשרים ו־ שלושה", "עשרים ו־
  שלושה", "שלושה־ רבעים"); the fractional tail after a rate follows "ו־ " as it follows "ו־"; evidence matching keeps every paragraph gap, so an excerpt quotes a blank line as a blank line, whether the line ends in "\n", "\r\n" or a bare "\r", and reads each run of whitespace once, in linear time; every whitespace character is horizontal space, a line end or a paragraph separator to the collapse and the bindings alike; and the readers that run on the raw text and the context classification see line ends and that wrap space as the cleaner does, so no spelling of the space after a maqaf changes what a text states, in grounding or in the recall inventory.
